{"text": "IASHVILLE\n\nQ. Virginia Slims is pulling out of the Nashville Tournament. Why?\n\nA. It was a marketing decision, a business decision. Attendance\nwasn\u2019t meeting our expectations and we've decided to focus our resources on\nNiet rand Il events. (Events in major metropolitan markets. The prize\nmoney is larger. Tier I - $550,000 Tier II ~ $350,000. They attract a\ndifferent level of player and a larger crowd.)\n\nQ. But Virginia Slims of Oklahoma is scheduled for next year, and\nthat\u2019s not a top tier event.\n\nA. True, but the decision was made too late to affect Oklahoma. We\ndidn\u2019t want to pull out at the last minute.\n\nQ. Are you dropping out because of the pressure against tobacco\nsponsorship of sporting events?\n\nA. No. Again, this is a business decision. There was never any\nanti-tobacco pressure in Nashville. We were warmly received by the\ncommunity - but the tournament doesn\u2019t fit in with our marketing plan.\n\nQ. How can you say there\u2019s no pressure to get out of tennis when\nyou've lost three other tournaments just in the last few months? (Indian\nWells, Albuquerque and Washington, D.C.) Isn/t this a trend?\n\nA. No. Those are three distinctly different situations.\n\nAlbuquerque - Like Nashville, Albuquerque does not fit into our\nmarketing plan. We were there for three years, but it wasn\u2019t performing up\nto our expectations. It was a business decision.\n\nIndian Wells - This also was a business decision. There were a number\nof issues which we couldn\u2019t come to agreement on with the promoter,\nInternational Management Group, so IMG chose to seek another sponsor. Keep\nin mind, however, that this is just Palm Springs/Indian Wells. Virginia\nSlims will continue to sponsor four major IMG-owned events in Chicago,\nHouston, Oakland and Philadelphia.\n\nOSPLTVEHOS", "words": [{"text": "IASHVILLE", "x0": 458, "y0": 94, "x1": 537, "y1": 105, "conf": 0.0}, {"text": "Q.", "x0": 104, "y0": 179, "x1": 120, "y1": 191, "c